{"meta": {"limit": 500, "next": null, "offset": 0, "previous": null, "total_count": 16}, "objects": [{"abstract": "This document defines the  Audit  Information  Transfer  Protocol (AITP),  a  simple transaction protocol suitable for transferring audit information between systems.  An initiator can use AITP  to request audit data from, or set audit management parameters at, a responder.  The responder handles the request and sets or returns the resulting data.  Both the amount of time required to generate the response and the amount of audit data returned  can  be  very large.   These are the main reasons for a unique protocol to support this type of service.", "ad": null, "expires": "1993-02-14T08:00:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 17008, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-dempsey-audit-info", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-dempsey-audit-info/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": [], "tags": [], "time": "1992-08-13T07:00:00Z", "title": "AUDIT INFORMATION TRANSFER PROTOCOL (AITP)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": 6359}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a Merkle-tree-based approach that can act as an\n   audit-layer detection mechanism to identify a malicious hub,\n   responsible for interoperable group communication between various\n   messaging platforms.  The proposed approach is based on the MIMI\n   protocol, which uses a central hub for timestamping and broadcasting\n   messages to clients operating on different platforms.  Even though\n   all MLS ciphertexts are end-to-end encrypted, they are routed through\n   the hub, making it a lucrative attack surface for message reordering\n   attacks.  To detect such attacks, the proposed approach suggests\n   creating Merkle proofs of messages and timestamps on the client-side,\n   which can subsequently be broadcast to other clients for verification\n   with local Merkle proofs.  The broadcast messages are encrypted too\n   and are sent probabilistically to avoid being dropped by the hub.  If\n   any of the proofs do not match, an alert is broadcast to the room,\n   indicating a malicious hub.  The approach has minimal communication\n   overhead for practical purposes.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-01-08T23:12:12Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146952, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-burger-mimi-audit-layer", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-burger-mimi-audit-layer/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153920/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-01-09T07:09:56Z", "title": "Detecting the Presence of a Malicious Hub in MIMI Protocol", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an architecture for auditing of agent-driven\n   interactions on the Internet.  Autonomous and semi-autonomous\n   software agents, including those based on artificial intelligence,\n   increasingly act on behalf of users, organizations, and services.\n   Existing auditing mechanisms often capture isolated system events but\n   do not consistently represent delegation relationships, user intent,\n   or evolving authorization.  In agent-driven systems, auditability\n   requires linking intent, delegation, authorization, and execution.\n   The proposed architecture enables this through distributed audit\n   record generation, propagation of audit context, optional\n   attestation, and additional logging for transparency.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-11T11:49:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156720, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kuehlewind-audit-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kuehlewind-audit-architecture/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163302/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168734/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-07T11:49:34Z", "title": "An Architecture for Auditing Agent Delegation and Interactions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Audit systems for autonomous agents commonly record the actions an\n   agent performed.  This makes the non-occurrence of a permitted action\n   unrepresentable: when nothing happens, there is no action to emit\n   anything.  This document describes an evidence model that records the\n   authorization decision rather than the action.  A decision exists\n   whether or not the action follows, so denials, expiries, and\n   commitments that were granted and never honoured remain\n   representable.\n\n   The document defines four disclosures that make a decision record\n   independently evaluable, an entry-emission rule for states a relying\n   party may need to reason about, and the evidentiary basis for claims\n   that a decision preceded its effect.  It distinguishes\n   correspondence, where two records agree about what happened, from\n   precedence, where the order of decision and effect is established,\n   and it requires records to state which of the two they carry.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-14T13:30:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160391, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-bradleyb-audit-decision-records", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-bradleyb-audit-decision-records/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167686/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-13T13:30:36Z", "title": "Signed Decision Records for Agent Authorization: Disclosures, Entry Emission, and Ordering Evidence", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a standard logging format for autonomous\n   AI agent systems.  The Agent Audit Trail (AAT) defines a\n   JSON-based record structure with mandatory fields for agent\n   identity, the authorizing principal, action classification,\n   outcome tracking, and trust level reporting.  Records are\n   linked via tamper-evident hash chaining using SHA-256 per\n   RFC 8785, with optional ECDSA signatures for non-repudiation.\n\n   The format addresses requirements from the EU AI Act\n   (Regulation 2024/1689), which mandates automatic recording of\n   events for high-risk AI systems, whose application dates were\n   staged into 2027-2028 by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744.  It also\n   maps informatively to SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria,\n   ISO/IEC 42001, the draft ISO/IEC 24970 and prEN 18229-1 logging\n   standards, and PCI DSS v4.0.1 logging requirements.\n\n   The design is transport-agnostic and supports export to JSONL,\n   Syslog (RFC 5424), and CSV while preserving chain integrity.\n   Privacy is addressed through input/output hashing, content\n   fingerprinting, and tombstone-based deletion compatible with\n   GDPR Article 17.\n\n   The -01 revision added pre-execution recording requirements,\n   recording independence, deny reason codes, replay protection,\n   external timestamp anchoring, and content fingerprinting based\n   on feedback from independent implementers.\n\n   The -02 revision added a Decision Reproducibility section\n   (Section 13) that distinguishes record reproducibility,\n   available for any model, from decision reproducibility,\n   available only for open-weight models executed at temperature\n   zero in an attested environment, and defines the associated\n   record fields.\n\n   The -03 revision added the Attestation Closure requirement\n   (Section 13.6): the digests recorded for decision\n   reproducibility MUST cover the complete computational closure\n   of the inference function -- model weights, tokenizer, chat\n   template, inference engine build, decoding configuration, and\n   numeric environment -- together with new record fields\n   (tokenizer_digest, chat_template_digest, engine_build_digest)\n   and a minimal-change threat analysis (Section 13.7) showing\n   that any component left outside the attested set is a forgery\n   channel.\n\n   The -04 revision added algorithm agility for post-quantum\n   signatures (ML-DSA-65, FIPS 204) alongside ECDSA P-256; a key\n   identifier (signer_kid, RFC 7638) that resolves which principal\n   signed an independently recorded record; a trust-level assignment\n   integrity requirement (Section 5.3) so that downgrading a\n   consequential action is an attributable event rather than silent\n   suppression; and OPTIONAL Merkle batch anchoring (Section 6.4)\n   using the RFC 6962 construction for compact inclusion proofs at\n   high throughput.  All -04 additions are OPTIONAL to produce, and\n   -04 verifiers stay backward compatible with -03: a -04 verifier\n   accepts -03 records, and the new signing metadata is required\n   only in records that carry a signature.\n\n   This revision (-07) adds a mandatory \"principal\" field\n   (Section 3.1) that binds each record to the party on whose\n   authority the agent acted; a Relationship to Other Work\n   section (Section 1.3) relating AAT to the WIMSE agent audit\n   record and authorization-evidence drafts and to SCITT\n   (RFC 9943); and corrections to the EU AI Act Article 12 and\n   Article 19 citations in the regulatory mapping (Section 11.1\n   and Appendix B).  Records produced under -06 and earlier\n   predate the \"principal\" field; verifiers processing\n   mixed-revision archives SHOULD treat its absence as \"principal\n   not recorded\" rather than a validation failure.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-29T17:46:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155757, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sharif-agent-audit-trail", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 65, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sharif-agent-audit-trail/", "rev": "09", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169473/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169652/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170134/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161719/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167875/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169090/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170274/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168530/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168657/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170991/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-26T17:46:36Z", "title": "Agent Audit Trail: A Standard Logging Format for Autonomous AI Systems", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   When an AI agent system is stopped, restricted or has its credentials\n   revoked during an incident, someone later decides whether it may run\n   again.  Incident response guidance asks for that decision to be\n   documented with the risk that remains, but no format says what the\n   record must contain for a party outside the deploying organization to\n   check it.  This document defines five record types for that purpose:\n   an incident action authorization, a revocation sweep, a re-enable\n   decision, a joint authority assignment for incidents that span\n   organizations, and a revocation boundary report.  Each is an in-toto\n   Statement carried in a DSSE envelope, uses the canonical form of the\n   agent audit record, and is checked by ordered rules with a named\n   reason for each failure.  A bundle rule set binds a re-enable\n   decision to the records it cites, by digest.  A published conformance\n   corpus carries a reject member for every rule that a member can\n   force.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T07:15:01Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162169, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gilda-audit-restoration-evidence", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gilda-audit-restoration-evidence/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171753/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T07:15:01Z", "title": "Restoration Evidence Records for AI Agent Incident Response", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a record format for AI agent authorization\r\n   decisions.  The format is one in-toto predicate type, signed inside a\r\n   DSSE envelope.  It carries the seven minimum audit fields that the\r\n   WIMSE AI Identity Management System framework requires, and two\r\n   properties that make those fields checkable: a canonicalization\r\n   contract, and both the authorization decision and the observed effect\r\n   with a derived three-valued agreement between them.  That framework\r\n   places the record format out of scope and takes no IANA action.  This\r\n   document supplies the format.  It defines no policy.\r\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-08T15:54:35Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161156, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gilda-wimse-agent-audit-record", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gilda-wimse-agent-audit-record/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/169554/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169291/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169836/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-05T15:54:35Z", "title": "An Audit Record Format for AI Agent Authorization Decisions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent auditing can rely on information produced by users, agents,\n   tools, services, policy components, and other systems across multiple\n   administrative domains.  The evidence needed to answer one auditing\n   question is not necessarily the evidence needed to answer another.\n   Successful verification of available evidence also does not, by\n   itself, establish that the evidence is applicable or sufficient for\n   the question being evaluated.\n\n   This document describes a representation-independent model for\n   stating an Audit Task, alternative evidence requirements for that\n   task, expectations about evidence production, and the basis for an\n   audit result.  It distinguishes verification of evidence from\n   evaluation of the Audit Task, missing evidence from a negative\n   observation, and validity of individual records from cross-domain\n   sufficiency.  It defines no audit record format, verification\n   mechanism, authorization model, completeness mechanism, storage\n   protocol, or auditing policy.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-19T06:11:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161654, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-sergeev-audit-task-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 41, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-sergeev-audit-task-requirements/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170443/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-16T06:11:55Z", "title": "Task-Relative Evidence Requirements for Agent Auditing", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a record format for AI agent authorization\n   decisions and the effects that followed them.  The format is one in-\n   toto predicate type, signed inside a DSSE envelope.  A record carries\n   the decision and the observed effect with a derived agreement between\n   them, whether the decision point could evaluate the request and how\n   fresh each input it relied on was, and a canonicalization contract\n   under which a party that trusts neither the agent nor its operator\n   recomputes the record's consistency from its bytes.  The document\n   also defines what a party that retains a record keeps beside it, so\n   that the record can later be dated from anchors that party does not\n   control.  The format carries the seven minimum audit fields of the\n   WIMSE AI Identity Management System framework, and it is offered as a\n   data model for the audit records the proposed AUDIT working group\n   would specify.  It defines no policy.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-02T22:20:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161671, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gilda-audit-agent-audit-record", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 71, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gilda-audit-agent-audit-record/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170487/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171029/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171062/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171266/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-29T22:20:04Z", "title": "An Audit Record Format for AI Agent Authorization Decisions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent audit records are signed, chained, counter-signed, timestamped,\n   and registered with transparency services.  Each mechanism\n   establishes a narrower fact than it is often taken to establish.\n   This document states, for any record format, protocol, or payment\n   method, what a check of an agent audit record can and cannot\n   establish about four properties: the independence of the entities\n   that signed or observed it, the completeness of a set of records, the\n   time at which a record or an action existed, and the result that a\n   verifier reports.  It defines independence by control rather than by\n   key or process separation, separates the independence of a signer\n   from the independence of an observation, states which commitments\n   cannot support a report that a set of records is complete, and\n   requires a verifier to report unauthenticated content as\n   unauthenticated.  It defines no record format, role, log, or service.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T13:13:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161939, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-audit-record-limits", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-audit-record-limits/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171055/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T13:13:18Z", "title": "What an Agent Audit Record Can and Cannot Establish", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Software agents act on authority that changes while they run:\n   permissions are granted and revoked, running instances are replaced,\n   and some requests are refused.  Activity logs record what a system\n   reports it did, but they do not by themselves show what an agent was\n   allowed to do when it acted, and the operator can edit them.  This\n   document specifies a model for an authority history: an append-only,\n   signed record of who granted what to whom, which actions were\n   admitted, which requests were refused, and which unfinished duties\n   survive when one agent replaces another.  Given independently\n   authenticated owner keys and history identity, a verifier can replay\n   the history and recompute its recorded authorization decisions while\n   trusting neither the agent nor the operator.  The document states\n   what such a verifier can and cannot establish.  Optional profiles\n   cover external anchoring, federated operation, human-attested keys\n   and zero-knowledge disclosure.  An informative appendix describes one\n   encoding.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T00:43:54Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162029, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-rampertab-audit-agent-authority-history", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 84, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-rampertab-audit-agent-authority-history/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171269/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T00:43:55Z", "title": "Replayable Authority Histories for Auditing Automated Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Audit records for software agents are designed to show what an agent\n   did.  Relying parties also ask a different question: whether an agent\n   could have used a capability at any time during a stated interval.\n   Records of actions cannot answer that question, because authority\n   that goes unused leaves no record of use, even if it existed for part\n   of the interval.\n\n   This document describes the problem of interval absence claims,\n   defines terms, lists requirements that an audit evidence system needs\n   to meet before it can support such claims, and gives test scenarios\n   that expose false claims of absence.  It does not define a protocol,\n   a data format, or a verification algorithm.  It is intended as input\n   to discussions of auditing for agent delegation and interactions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T08:57:57Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162104, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-baek-audit-absence-claims", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-baek-audit-absence-claims/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171524/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T08:57:57Z", "title": "Interval Absence Claims in Agent Auditing: Problem Statement, Requirements, and Test Scenarios", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The proposed AUDIT working group is scoped to define interoperable\n   mechanisms for auditing the observable behavior of agents across\n   trust boundaries, and its draft charter names Audit Data Models and\n   Semantics as a Standards Track deliverable.  This document presents\n   one candidate for that deliverable: the AER-1 execution receipt,\n   specified in draft-zambo-aer1-26, read as an audit data model.  It\n   summarizes the receipt record by reference to the AER-1\n   specification, shows how the record covers the audit scope the draft\n   charter describes (tool interactions, invoked and declined actions,\n   subagent and tool calls across domains, and records verifiable by a\n   party that trusts neither the agent nor its operator), records the\n   conformance corpus and independent implementation status behind the\n   model, and states the limits of the model plainly.  This document\n   defines no new format and changes no AER-1 field, digest\n   construction, or verification rule.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-04T12:48:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162108, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zambo-aer1-audit-data-model", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zambo-aer1-audit-data-model/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171542/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-31T12:48:29Z", "title": "The AER-1 Execution Receipt as an Audit Data Model for Agent Interactions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent auditing relies on distributed enforcement points (EPs) to\n   observe and intervene in agent activities.  This document analyzes\n   Agent, Gateway, and Host EPs by detailing their coverage and audit\n   data.  This provides a basis for understanding the capabilities of\n   different EPs and evaluating overall audit evidence coverage.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T13:00:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162201, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liu-audit-enforcement-points", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liu-audit-enforcement-points/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171875/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T13:00:14Z", "title": "Enforcement Points for Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The architecture for auditing agent delegation and interactions\n   proposed for the AUDIT effort names Interaction Records, Action\n   Records and Authorization Transition Records, and leaves their\n   encoding open.  This document shows how an existing signed record,\n   the decision receipt of the open-source proofbundle project, together\n   with its companion outcome receipt, gives three such records exact\n   bytes, a signature and digest bindings that a third party can check\n   offline.  The three cases are a human approval, a declined action and\n   an executed action with its result.  For each case the document maps\n   the receipt members onto the record types of the architecture, states\n   what the receipt adds and what it does not show, and gives an offline\n   verification procedure.  It states one requirement for approvals,\n   that an approval is honoured at most once, also after the record of\n   consumed approvals is rotated.  Test vectors for all three cases,\n   each with a counterexample, are included.  The document also\n   describes, informatively, how a receipt can be registered with a\n   Transparency Service under RFC 9943 and what a SCITT Receipt then\n   shows and does not show.  This document defines no new format,\n   predicate or registry.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T22:23:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162146, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-gruszka-audit-decision-receipts", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 55, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-gruszka-audit-decision-receipts/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171692/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171751/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171766/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171792/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172008/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172188/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T22:23:55Z", "title": "Decision Receipts for Approvals, Declined Actions and Executed Actions in Agent Audit Records", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   AI agents converse with people, other agents and tools.  There is a\n   growing need to capture these conversations for audit, compliance and\n   review.  This document sorts the requirements for capturing agent\n   conversations into those that fit vCon, those better met by plain\n   logging and those that need other solutions.  It then identifies the\n   gaps in vCon and proposes a strawman set of vCon extensions to fill\n   them.  It is intended as a discussion document.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T20:27:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162279, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-petrie-vcon-agent-audit-roadmap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 14, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-petrie-vcon-agent-audit-roadmap/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172116/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T20:27:25Z", "title": "vCon for AI Agent Conversations: Requirements, Gaps and Extension Roadmap", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}]}